<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=vacuum-pump">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vacuum pump</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1650">1650</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as air pump</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A vacuum pump draws gas particles from a sealed volume to leave a partial vacuum. The first was invented in 1650 by Otto von Guericke, preceded by the suction pump, which dates to antiquity, and by Evangelista Torricelli's mercury <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barometer">barometer</a> of 1643, which he argued held a vacuum at its top. Four years later Guericke conducted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> hemispheres experiment, showing that teams of horses could not separate two hemispheres from which the air had been evacuated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-boyle">Robert Boyle</a> improved the design, with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-hooke">Robert Hooke</a>, and by 1709 Francis Hauksbee had a two-cylinder pump. Heinrich Geissler's mercury displacement pump of 1855 reached a record vacuum of about 10 Pa.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1650</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Otto von Guericke</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The suction pump that preceded it dates to antiquity: dual-action suction pumps were found at Pompeii, and al-Jazari described them in the 13th century. These did not produce a measurable vacuum, which was first understood in the 17th century.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/barometer">Barometer<span>1644</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/thermodynamics">Thermodynamics<span>1824</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/electron">Electron<span>1897</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=vacuum-pump">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
